#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091967 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/id #uuid "c3aac9bd-c462-5664-a196-278a9671fbbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4243" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {32},\\: \\dfrac 1 4,\\: 2,\\: 16,\\: \\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=\\dfrac{1}{32}$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {4}\\right)} {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {32} \\right)} = 8.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{64}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{8}^{2}}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{-2}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{n-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{n-3} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {8}^{n-3} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({8}^{n-3}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(8) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\, 000)} {\\log (8)} + 3 \\approx 9.643.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091968 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/id #uuid "4af09b88-5f57-572f-ae64-bfe64e94450d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4244" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n16,\\: 8,\\: 4,\\: 2,\\: \\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=16,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 8 {16}  = \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{5-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,000\\,1.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,000\\,1 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n5 - \\dfrac { \\log (0.000\\, 000\\, 1)} {\\log (2)} \\approx 28.253.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $29$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091969 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/id #uuid "154a5006-6c4f-58c7-a228-ccbefabd5050" :question/math-academy-id "e-4245" :question/problem "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $54,$ and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,000?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that $a_{4}= 54$ and  $r= 3,$ we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for $a_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{a_n&=a_1\\cdot r^{n-1}\\[3pt]\n\t\ta_4&=a_1\\cdot 3^{4-1}\\[3pt]\n\t54&=a_1\\cdot 3^3\\[3pt]\n\t54&=27a_1\\[3pt]\n\ta_1&=2\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >1\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >500 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(3)^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (500)} {\\log (3)} + 1 \\approx 6.657.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "802d1919-d991-5b23-a95b-dcc4f411683a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340140} {:answer/id #uuid "302f24d1-251c-5209-9326-8d0f6b6201b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $11$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340141} {:answer/id #uuid "289994cd-0678-59f5-8055-b6ec05801911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $9$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340142} {:answer/id #uuid "eda0303e-e677-5a08-8d2f-369282175074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340143} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9e2fe7-78be-52b4-ba0d-dab9e55836a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $8$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eda0303e-e677-5a08-8d2f-369282175074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340143} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a2a184-0a0b-533f-b7c7-beb3b335f7f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e846278-0be5-5403-b98f-fa7b7f911856" :question/math-academy-id "q-218498" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,08$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n256,\\,64,\\,16,\\,4,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=256$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{64}{256}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =256\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{4}\\cdot {4}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={4}^{5-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,08.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,08 \\\\ {4}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,08 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}≈11.805\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,08.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c32e71dd-28eb-54d3-97e4-9abdefb87f01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c32e71dd-28eb-54d3-97e4-9abdefb87f01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340147} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a83989-7eb7-504c-9634-dd2b08c1d7bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ff86720-a67d-52b4-8d07-8aaca98e7ea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-270976" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds  $2\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,10,\\,15,\\,\\frac{45}{2},\\,\\frac{135}{4},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $2\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2000 \\\\ 10\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >2000 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >200 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(200) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(200) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(200)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(200)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(200)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1≈14.067.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $15$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "938310c5-7318-51ff-b48f-81d22cf4f0f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340150} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3c0c07-f3e7-5ad2-a892-aee17b377bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "938310c5-7318-51ff-b48f-81d22cf4f0f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340150} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa22889a-e180-5804-af5a-616418acce8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f764e4f-dcbe-509f-94dd-fe5956c0700e" :question/math-academy-id "q-277931" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n2,\\,8,\\,32,\\,128,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $58\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >58\\,000 \\\\ 2\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >58\\,000 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >29\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1≈8.412.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $58\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b573e779-a669-5f7a-a60e-9435e18d26c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b573e779-a669-5f7a-a60e-9435e18d26c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340154} :answer-field/id #uuid "71d4bb9a-012b-5d7a-9e8e-b7b9477e8bb7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "568329f9-94a5-5f05-8083-8e44e7e97d7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-277935" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds  $30$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n2,\\,\\frac{8}{3},\\,\\frac{32}{9},\\,\\frac{128}{27},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $30$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{\\frac{8}{3}}{2}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $30.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >30 \\\\ 2\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >30 \\\\ {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >15 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(15) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(15) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(15)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(15)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(15)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})}+1≈10.413.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $11$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $30.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ffb06a5-4519-5fe9-a186-57abd31900c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340157} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d673c0-634b-56d6-abf0-4f5f64fe767b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ffb06a5-4519-5fe9-a186-57abd31900c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340157} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9cc2721-3351-5e17-b1bc-a41a1fe19b1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60def6d0-7335-5fef-a296-c2105e0a1600" :question/math-academy-id "q-277964" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds  $9\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n3,\\,6\\sqrt{7},\\,84,\\,168\\sqrt{7},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $9\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=3$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{6\\sqrt{7}}{3}=2\\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $9\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >9\\,000 \\\\ 3\\cdot {(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1} & >9\\,000 \\\\ {(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1} & >3\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({(2\\sqrt{7})}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\sqrt{7})}+1≈5.805.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $6$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $9\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e15ea815-80fe-5f41-b8f0-cf6d04177ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e15ea815-80fe-5f41-b8f0-cf6d04177ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340161} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b06e354-b06c-5291-93e2-1eb43a0ddc9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a3d135c-3c09-5a35-b1f3-27acd9c7837f" :question/math-academy-id "q-277971" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,31$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n125,\\,25,\\,5,\\,1,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.000\\,31$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=125$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{25}{125}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={5}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={5}^{4-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,31.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,31 \\\\ {5}^{4-n} & <0.000\\,31 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{4-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31) \\\\ (4-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31) \\\\ 4-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-4 \\\\ n & >4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}≈9.020.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,31.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e91597f-d0b4-547b-a358-e75e3ee2ab64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e91597f-d0b4-547b-a358-e75e3ee2ab64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340164} :answer-field/id #uuid "69a459ad-4afb-5faa-afe3-4007a1a84362" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ef379ee-1cf9-5420-a7f1-35b3b4d831c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-278053" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.004$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n20,\\,4,\\,\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\frac{4}{25},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.004$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=20$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{4}{20}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =20{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.004.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.004 \\\\ 20{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <0.004 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.004}{20} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})}+1≈6.292.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.004.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f92173f0-767e-5928-9d9d-c25e82721ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f92173f0-767e-5928-9d9d-c25e82721ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340167} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1250a89-01c6-58e3-8390-be66cc05a9e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cdf5899-02fb-5458-b322-2c659223d357" :question/math-academy-id "q-278078" :question/problem "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $972$, and the common ratio is $3.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $30\\,000$ is the{{field-1}}th term." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=972$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 972 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ 972 & =243{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{972}{243} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $30\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >30\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >7\\,500 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1≈9.122.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $30\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98af2b7e-b501-50b4-9ff1-67ef6405e85b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $11$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090306} {:answer/id #uuid "9b59777f-2b7f-5144-8388-3b195f6d0e40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $13$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090307} {:answer/id #uuid "a7226361-d652-5ef0-8f65-dd0f631aa6f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090308} {:answer/id #uuid "08f8122f-3138-592b-a3dc-3baba00ee74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $14$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090309} {:answer/id #uuid "364d0936-9952-5d0b-a27d-353a972d10cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7226361-d652-5ef0-8f65-dd0f631aa6f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090308} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b26187a-009e-5acc-9d13-e1a43587b264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "904d2ffd-5671-50ed-98c6-a20c95b996b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-39655" :question/problem "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $5$, and the $6$th term is $40.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$2\\,020?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=5$ and ${a}_{6}=40$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{40}{5} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{3}=5$ and  $r=2$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{3-1} \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =4{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{n-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,020.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {2}^{n-3} & >2\\,020 \\\\ {2}^{n-3} & >404 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{2}^{n-3} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3≈11.658\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,020.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ee4cfe-b127-5753-87e5-d3e4b946b8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $24$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340170} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2c9fec-97fa-5417-b21c-a662d068c855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $28$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340171} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ce4caf-16f3-5c5c-a6d4-d270a70f0c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $25$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340172} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7594d0-c4e6-5d65-a1b6-38749fd72763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $26$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340173} {:answer/id #uuid "14a4b541-807a-5b7c-aac7-e026d93e2d1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $27$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f7594d0-c4e6-5d65-a1b6-38749fd72763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $26$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340173} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c8248d-8697-508c-92e3-72f547b81de5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71e1875b-fe8a-53a0-ac17-ab2cc533cc26" :question/math-academy-id "q-39656" :question/problem "The $2$nd term of a geometric sequence with a positive common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}$, and the $4$th term is $\\frac{2}{3}.$ Given that all terms of the sequence are positive, what is the first term of this sequence to be less than\n$0.000\\,11?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{2}{3}$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{4-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}}{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the common ratio is positive, we take the positive value. Now, since ${a}_{4}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and  $r=\\frac{2}{3}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-1} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,11.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n} & <0.000\\,11 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11) \\\\ (3-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11) \\\\ 3-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}-3 \\\\ n & >3-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n3-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}≈25.480\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $26$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ca2306-a0dc-5301-b33a-c2b9fb5118c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $15$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090930} {:answer/id #uuid "c1598589-6d36-5127-a7ce-d5057440dde0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $19$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090931} {:answer/id #uuid "11318dd3-6978-5383-81d1-1777eea3fa66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $17$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090932} {:answer/id #uuid "3aed3e82-25aa-5e5e-8035-0fbb6a87a554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $16$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090933} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8d8940-b56e-51d8-a584-a60201f3be0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $18$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b8d8940-b56e-51d8-a584-a60201f3be0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $18$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090934} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4bef4ca-37c5-53f8-bf3b-270622ba9970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96f03ca4-722a-50b8-895c-e2999102c436" :question/math-academy-id "q-67493" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,6$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,32,\\,16,\\,8,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{32}{64}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{6}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{7-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,6.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,6 \\\\ {2}^{7-n} & <0.000\\,6 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(2)}^{7-n} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6) \\\\ (7-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6) \\\\ 7-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-7 \\\\ n & >7-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n7-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}≈17.703\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $18$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e502ced-08b3-5340-a615-c13d8d091055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $24$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340177} {:answer/id #uuid "a7857d10-afc2-5c24-862d-f9656fd05ba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $21$st term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340178} {:answer/id #uuid "793ece86-36bf-5365-bc51-85e69c4e2775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $20$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340179} {:answer/id #uuid "c700da3f-4608-59d5-b504-b7a00c378221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $23$rd term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340180} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec7d735-0cdf-5587-b454-bbe26ddfcab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $22$nd term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "793ece86-36bf-5365-bc51-85e69c4e2775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $20$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340179} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7b8b27-f192-559d-a690-9b47a5a98c81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffcedc42-4166-50a9-b8c0-b873fc94aa46" :question/math-academy-id "q-67495" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.027$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n54,\\,36,\\,24,\\,16,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=54$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{36}{54}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =54\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.027.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.027 \\\\ 54\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <0.027 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.027}{54} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1≈19.746\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $20$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.027.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bcf0a3b-7b87-584a-b2dc-e9cc66f10c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $14$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090936} {:answer/id #uuid "cae41a6d-f961-5834-965d-2b1dc16b316c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $11$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090937} {:answer/id #uuid "013ccd5f-838e-573b-8d59-58724828eb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090938} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2f3602-3e4b-5c26-9290-d8dbabc50410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090939} {:answer/id #uuid "3f944d53-233c-5660-820f-e5d9cdbc8f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $13$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c2f3602-3e4b-5c26-9290-d8dbabc50410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090939} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e567fb7-6ea3-5841-8e6a-01127163bbf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4eae977c-d3cf-5ee9-8954-e478b07b3011" :question/math-academy-id "q-67515" :question/problem "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $108$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,984?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=108$ and  $r=\\frac{3}{2}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4-1} \\\\ 108 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3} \\\\ 108 & =\\frac{27}{8}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,984.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >1\\,984 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >62 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1≈11.179\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,984.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de37ca97-8d32-55fa-bec5-23259f6b3f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $13$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340184} {:answer/id #uuid "2238cb17-c4e3-5ef5-b308-36afdb328eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $14$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340185} {:answer/id #uuid "46045125-2351-550d-89ee-bb204b3325c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340186} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ea24e9-be9c-5044-9968-f1f0d4cdedb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340187} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9cac66-0c10-570a-8353-6df0a42c4229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $11$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6ea24e9-be9c-5044-9968-f1f0d4cdedb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340187} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d93ee3d-ed35-5d57-8995-95300e1bd213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69c32776-2f0c-51de-af80-ceee4b997fc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-67516" :question/problem "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $3$, and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$907?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=3$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 3 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{3}^{4}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{-4}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{n-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $907.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n-5} & >907 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(3)}^{n-5} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ (n-5)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ n-5 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5≈11.199\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $907.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfcdc615-a344-535e-a334-f66eba459802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $9$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340191} {:answer/id #uuid "6613d21a-d7a7-511c-9137-2445c99b3453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $6$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340192} {:answer/id #uuid "93a95c5a-6afd-5e7b-91d0-e8b9e3a0d5ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340193} {:answer/id #uuid "584b36cf-bb74-5e42-901b-c6248be5a715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $8$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340194} {:answer/id #uuid "d6945fde-81bd-5a18-9c4f-b0e156c5aebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $7$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584b36cf-bb74-5e42-901b-c6248be5a715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $8$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340194} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea7c083d-a706-5de2-8fd5-9be67ad0c2d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b75ad58-0281-53ce-b9c6-78ac66c07398" :question/math-academy-id "q-9743" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n4,\\,28,\\,196,\\,1\\,372,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=4$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{28}{4}=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {7}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 4\\cdot {7}^{n-1} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {7}^{n-1} & >500\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({7}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(7) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1≈7.744\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$"}]}
